<!--Post: 10559--><!--Time: 1110002220-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST10559"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Next<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Next<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 04, 2005 - 9:57 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->This was on Court TV web page: <BR>&#34;The 46 minutes of news conference self congratulations on &#34;catching&#34; BTK seems a bit misplaced considering that after 30 years of so-called investigation, police were not even able to tie three victims &#40;Wegerle, Hedge and Davis&#41; to BTK.  It&#39;s hard to understand how so much investigative effort on the part of Wichita police and the FBI failed to respond to the obvious clues in Rader&#39;s past that tied in with the profile that had been developed for BTK: <BR> <BR>He went to Wichita State University, where one BTK letter was photocopied and a Professor P.J. Wyatt had exposed in her classes the poem &#34;Oh, Death&#34; from which BTK created one of his poems. <BR>He was in the Air Force. It was long speculated that BTK got the letters from &#34;Born To Kill,&#34; a USAF squadron term. He may have met BTK victim Joseph Otero, also in the Air Force at that time. <BR>He worked at Coleman&#39;s, where two other victims worked <BR>He is an odd guy with a need to exert power and control as evidenced by the code compliance position he held with the Park City government. Several of his neighbors have gone on the record calling him a bureaucratic &#34;bully.&#34; This type of behavior is consistent with a sadistic serial killer and should have been a red flag to investigators. <BR>He lived nearby some victims, even on the same street as one of them.&#34; <BR> <BR> What were the PD and FBI doing? They had acess to all this info. Un-freakin-believable. <BR> <BR>I guarantee the more info that gets out, the worse the whole investigation will look, and it looks very bad already. I can&#39;t believe that press conference they had. Don&#39;t they know how bad they look? <BR> <BR>Attention all serial killers - Move to Wichita if you don&#39;t want to get caught.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 10559-->
<!--Post: 11566--><!--Time: 1110913850-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11566"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Zenith<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Zenith<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Tuesday, March 15, 2005 - 11:10 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think everyone&#39;s trying to be respectful of law enforcement and hold out some hope that they somehow did the right things in this case. It&#39;s hard to know since they have so much information that we&#39;re not privvy to.  <BR> <BR>When the trial starts, that&#39;s when we&#39;ll be able to draw more accurate conclusions about their work. <BR>  <BR>But I have to agree with you, I viewed the overwhelmingly long news conference where they spent almost all of their time patting themselves on the back for their dedication and hard work, and I came away dismayed. It was a time for BRIEFLY congratulating those who worked so hard on the case, and then spending the majority of the time and focus on the victims and their families. It was odd to say the least. <BR> <BR>I recall mentioning to a friend that I wouldn&#39;t be breaking my arm patting myself on the back if it had taken me 30 years to solve a crime, and I only then solved it because the dumb ass sent me a computer disk with his name on it........<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11566-->
<!--Post: 11796--><!--Time: 1111181342-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11796"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Friday, March 18, 2005 - 1:29 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->To be fair to LE they had to proceed over the last few years with great caution. They had to be absoluteley 100% sure that they got as much proof/evidence possible before making an arrest warrant. They had to be sure or they might have had happen to SFPD happen in this case: <BR> <BR>In the Zodiac case enough information was put together for a search warrant on the prime suspect, at that time, by SFPD. Unfortunately SFPD acted hastily and only received a warrant for one location. The suspect had multiple homes and multiple trailers which in retrospect should have all been searched.  .  .  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11796-->
<!--Post: 11834--><!--Time: 1111246387-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11834"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 19, 2005 - 7:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I don&#39;t know, Tac. You said, &#34;To be fair to LE they had to proceed over the last few years with great caution. They had to be absoluteley 100% sure that they got as much proof/evidence possible before making an arrest warrant.&#34; <BR> <BR>Try telling that to Wichitas&#39; poor Mr. Vasquez. His reputation was wiped out last Dec. by the cops bogus BTK arrest. It was made on the flimsiest evidence. They also forced him then &amp; there in his living room to be swabbed for DNA. He&#39;s now suing them for millions of dollars. <BR> <BR>But I guess 31 years IS being pretty cautious to arrest a vicious serial torture-murderer. And then only after he handed them all the evidence they needed several times on a &#39;silver platter&#39;.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11834-->
<!--Post: 11835--><!--Time: 1111247579-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11835"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:vscantu1@msn.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Vscantu<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Vscantu<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 19, 2005 - 7:52 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Correction: The above gentlemans&#39; name who was wrongly arrested as BTK is Roger Valadez.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11835-->
<!--Post: 11846--><!--Time: 1111278602-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11846"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Tac<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Tac<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->11-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 19, 2005 - 4:30 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->V, <BR> <BR>Point well taken now let me remove the sword from my chest and respond.  .  .   <BR> <BR>The Wichita police obviously made a huge mistake when they went after Mr/ Valadez with &#34;the flimsiest evidence.&#34; I agree they blew it in this instance and probably many others as well. <BR> <BR>I am sure LE made mistakes during the last 31 years but they also did not have DNA technology and other new forensic techniques to work with back in the 1970s. In addition this is a very small city with little media attention. The lack of attention helped hide BTK as well as LE&#39;s mistakes throughout the investigation.  <BR> <BR>Imagine if BTK was based in NYC or Chicago ? How many times would LE have made hasty warrants due to incredible media pressure in order to try to capture or &#34;frame&#34; someone ?  <BR> <BR>My point was that after having their nose rubbed in it for 30&#43; yrs that they would most likely try to take in all evidence before making hasty search warrants or accusations.  .  .  Obviously I did not take into account LE&#39;s level of desperation in part due to the renewed focus of national media attention both print, internet and television.  .  .   <BR> <BR>It is easy to criticize until you have actually been a cop and walked in their shoes.  .  .  everyone else always thinks they can do a better job IMO.  .  .  <!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11846-->
<!--Post: 11868--><!--Time: 1111305185-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11868"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:btk@mikecole.org" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Mike_cole<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Mike_cole<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->8-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, March 19, 2005 - 11:53 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->The news conference was a total embarrassment.  I looked forward to it for 12 hours and was disgusted by it after 15 minutes. <BR> <BR>The fact of the matter is that DR was caught due to one fact and one fact alone: he was determined to act until he got caught.  BTK was caught in spite of LE&#39;s investigatory techniques, not because of them. Had DR had the discipline to do nothing, he would have died without LE ever learning his identity. <BR> <BR>Whenever given a choice, WPD chose to withhold information and &#34;protect the public from itself&#34;.  The problem is, as public servants, they had a responsibility to be as open and forthcoming with the public as reasonably possible.  IMO, they performed &#40;and continue to perform&#41; dismally with respect to this responsibility.  Not surprisingly, they reaped what they sowed for 30 years...  When all is said and done, I suspect the BTK investigation will be a brilliant example of what not to do. <BR> <BR>And on top of all that, Chief Williams managed to create grammatically-challenged headlines: &#34;BTK is arrested&#34;.  Maybe he should have run that by somebody.  &#34;BTK is under arrest&#34; or &#34;BTK has been arrested&#34; perhaps... <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11868-->
<!--Post: 11894--><!--Time: 1111363250-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST11894"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, March 20, 2005 - 4:00 pm: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Not to mention that after all of their &#34;case building&#34;, so to speak, and &#34;protecting&#34; the evidence by keeping it from the public, They have apparently made a monumental, yet elementry,  mistake by obtaining the daughter&#39;s DNA without first obtaining the proper legal authorization, thus placing all evidence obtained susequently in jeapardy of being thrown out.  <BR> <BR>Since there has been virtually no mention of this particular problem in the local news lately, I suspect that now they are busy compounding the mistake by trying to suppress the media as they &#34;bargain&#34; with Raders&#39;s daughter or other other principles in the case to regain their legal footing. <BR> <BR>Personally I wouldn&#39;t be opposed to lynching him, considering what he has done and the fact that I have yet to hear him deny that he did any of it &#40;other than in formal, legal terms [which means nothing]&#41;.  <BR> <BR>This case, however, from the start, has been handled primarily by polititions and their lackeys, rather than investigators and will be huge black eye for Wichita and Kansas law enforcement which has historically been, although at times administered by gentlemen of questionable pedigree, efficient, reliable and &#40;where public enemies are concerned&#41; unconcerned with politics and economic status.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
<!--/Post: 11894-->
</table></td></tr></table>
